-- title:
--   PSL₂(ℤ) is the free product C₂ * C₃
-- statement:
--   The assertion is the existence of a group isomorphism $e$ from the free product (coproduct in the category of groups, `Monoid.CoprodI`) of the family indexed by $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$ whose $i$-th factor is the group $\mathbb{Z}/(i+2)\mathbb{Z}$ written multiplicatively — so the factors are cyclic of orders $2$ and $3$ — onto the quotient of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, the group of $2 \times 2$ integer matrices of determinant $1$, by its centre, subject to two normalisations: $e$ sends the canonical image in the free product of the element $1$ of the $0$-th factor $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ (transported through `Multiplicative.ofAdd`, i.e. the generator of the factor of order $2$) to the class modulo the centre of $S = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 0\end{pmatrix}$, and sends the canonical image of the element $1$ of the first factor $\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}$ (the generator of the factor of order $3$) to the class modulo the centre of $ST = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$. No hypotheses are assumed. The centre of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ being $\{\pm 1\}$, the quotient is $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$.
--
--   This is the classical presentation of the modular group: $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ is the free product of the cyclic groups generated by the images of $S$ and $ST$, of orders $2$ and $3$. It underlies the combinatorial analysis of functions on $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$-orbits used in the numerical work on modular curves, and is cited in the computation of ranks of spaces of additive maps and of orbit counts for the $S$, $ST$ action.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularGroup_exists_mulEquiv_freeProduct_quotient_center.lean

